## 1. Introduction
Most of the organic dyes, due to their toxic and carcinogenic properties, are important threats for living creatures in water as well as human health. Moreover, almost all dyes are not biodegradable and are resistant to environmental conditions; therefore, they cause major problems in wastewater treatment [1,2]. Sometimes, dyes are classified according to their application or even solubility. Acidic, basic, direct, and reactive dyes are examples of soluble dyes while azo, dispersed, sulfuric, and pulp dyes are examples of insoluble dyes [3]. Methylene blue that is needed in the textile industry is one of the most well-known water pollutants that cause many health problems such as abdominal disorders, respiratory problems, skin allergies, and blindness. Also, this dye harms the environment and damages the ecosystem balance. Methylene blue inhibits light penetration for aquatic creatures and threatens their life. As a result, its removal from water is the most important mission to preserve the ecosystem and environment [4]. Nowadays, many studies are conducted to achieve an ideal method to remove dye to reuse wastewaters. The existing method to remove dye can be divided into three categories of biological, chemical, and physical filtrations. Among these methods, the physical method for dye removal is the most common. These methods are selected due to their simplicity and efficiency. Among the countless methods of dye removal, adsorption due to its significant ability in removing all materials, low cost, various applied applications, simple design [5,6], simple operation, low harmful secondary products, a wide spectrum of adsorbent, and simple adsorbent reconstruction [7] is one of the superior techniques for dye removal. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a group of crystalline micro-porous materials introduced in the 1990s [8]. These materials include single metal ions or cluster metal ions that are bound by organic ligands to create a wide and crystalline framework. This crystalline allows the X diffraction technique to provide accurate structural information about MOFs [9]. MOFs are developed as a large group of crystalline materials with high porosity [10], high surface area, and high thermal stability [11,12]. MOFs allow flexible structural designs to have pores, surface area, and properties consistent with various components. This property has attracted many researchers. Up to now, more than 2000 various MOF structures are reported and studied [13]. These materials, due to porous structures, high chemical stability [1], and various active sites, are suitable adsorbents or photocatalysts for water treatment. In organic and metal frameworks classification, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) with zeolite topologies have been widely taken into consideration in past decades. For example, various studies confirm the efficiency of ZIFs as suitable adsorbents, catalysts, and gas separators. ZIFs are porous combined materials with structures similar to zeolites that are constructed on foursquare networks of $\mathsf{M}=(\mathsf{C u},\mathsf{C o},\mathsf{Z n},...)$ MN4bounded to imidazolate ligands [14]. ZIF-8 has been widely studied. This MOF has sodalite topography with specific properties, easy preparation, and wide applications [15]. There are several methods to prepare ZIF such as synthesis at room temperature, hydrothermal, solo thermal, sonochemical, mechanical chemical, electrochemical, and microwave. Among these methods, synthesis at room temperature is an easy method, since it does not need complicated tests and is carried out quickly. Moreover, this method consumed less energy relative to other common methods such as thermal solvent and sonochemical. Therefore, crystallization at room temperature is a powerful way to prepare ZIFs in different sizes. According to the important effect of particle size on properties and applications of nanoparticles, effective synthesis methods that provide control over particle size are desirable. The integration of MOFs with various applied materials is an effective strategy to modify MOF performance by introducing new capabilities for practical applications [16]. Carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC) is a colorless, odorless, and non-toxic powder soluble in water [17] that functions with carboxymethyl anion and hydroxyl functional groups as a context for reactions with other polymers and provides actives sizes to adsorb pollutants [18]. Zeolites are crystalline hydrated alumina silicate materials with uniform pores [19] and significant adsorbents. Also, they are considered as selective adsorbents [20] and have good ion exchange properties and molecular adsorption, large surface area, and hydrophobicity property for other pollutants and to remove dyes [20,21].
Hollow beads based on biopolymers are fabricated by the covalent or electrostatic wrapping of colloidal spherical particles with polymeric layers followed by removal of the core-templates by dissolution or chemical treatment. In this study, we investigate a novel process for the cultivation of self-assembled carboxymethyl cellulose hollow beads without the use of a core-template which yields spherical hollow beads based on metal-ion derivatized carboxymethyl cellulose prepared by partial etherification of the cellulose hydroxyl groups, contains metal-binding carboxylate groups and a large fraction of hydroxyl groups capable of forming hydrogen bonds. Here, carboxymethyl cellulose-based ZSM-5/Zeolitic imidazolate framework (CMC/ZSM-5/ ZIF-8) hollow bionanocomposite beads with different compositions (CMC, CMC/ZIF-8, CMC/ZSM-5 zeolite, and CMC/ZIF-8/ZSM-5 zeolite composites) were synthesized and employed as an adsorbent for methylene blue removal from aqueous solution in batch and continuous fixed bed systems. FESEM, FTIR, XRD, and BET measurements have been employed for characterizing the synthetic bionanocomposite and these techniques indicated that the bionanocomposites have been favorably synthesized. The kinetics, concentration effect on adsorption processes, and regeneration and reuse investigations were performed.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Hexavalent iron chloride (Chem-lab Co.), methylene blue, 2- methylimidazolate, and hexavalent zinc nitrate (Merck Co.), sodium carboxymethyl cellulose (Cermat Co.) were used without no further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8)
The required amounts of hexavalent zinc nitrate $(0.5~\mathrm{g})$ and 2- methyl imidazolate $(4.83~\mathrm{~g})$ with a molar ratio of 1:35:1280 (hexavalent zinc nitrate: 2-methyl imidazolate: ethanol) were added separately to 25 and $15\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol. The solution containing zinc nitrate
$6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ was added to the solution containing 2-methyl imidazole and stirred for $20\mathrm{h}$ with a magnetic stirrer. After $20\mathrm{h}$ , the resulting milky solution was centrifuged three times each for $20\mathrm{min}$ and was washed by methanol or water. Finally, the remaining matter was dried in an oven for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $60^{\circ}C$ (Fig. 1).
## 2.3. Synthesis of CMC/ZSM-5/ZIF-8 bionanocomposite beads
The cultivation of hollow, metal derivatized carboxymethyl cellulose beads comprises the dropwise addition of the sodium carboxymethyl cellulose gel to the iron (III) solution and instantly, within the spherical droplets are assembled as a result of interfacial phenomena. Four biocomposite beads with different compositions of ZSM-5 and ZIF-8 were prepared (CMC/ZSM-5/ZIF-8 with 5:5, CMC/ZSM-5 with 10:0, CMC/ZIF-8 with 0:10, and CMC with 0:0 weight percentage contents of ZSM-5: ZIF-8). To prepare the adsorbent beads, a $25\mathrm{mL}$ gel solution of CMC with $80~\mathrm{g/L}$ concentration in deionized water was prepared and a certain amount of ZSM-5 zeolite and the synthesized ZIF-8 was added to the CMC solution. Each mixture was placed on a stirrer $(1500~\mathrm{rpm})$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then, placed into an ultrasonic bath for debobbling the prepared gel. Then, the resulted gel was added dropwise through a syringe with a flow rate of $0.2\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{min}$ to a $2\mathsf{M}$ hexavalent iron chloride solution under stirring $(200\mathrm{rpm})$ to prepare beads. To achieve sustainability, the beads were stirred for $20\mathrm{min}$ inside the cross-linking iron chloride solution. During this period and as interfacial coprecipitation reactions between the carboxylate groups and the iron ions were building up the walls of the hollow beads. The resulted grains were separated, washed by distilled water, and kept in distilled water for 2 days. After 2 days, the grains were dry completely (Fig. 1). To investigation of suitable concentration of cross-linker (iron ions) and CMC solutions for prepare stable beads, concentrations of 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.2, 0.5, 1, and $2\mathrm{{M}}$ of hexavalent iron chloride and 20, 40, and ${80}\ \mathrm{g/L}$ of CMC gel solutions were prepared.
## 2.4. Characterization
Field Scanning Electron Microscopy (MIRA3) was used for the investigation of morphological and surface characteristics. The structural properties of the samples were identified using X-ray Diffraction (PW1730). Fourier Transform Infrared Spectrometer (AVATAR) was applied to study the functional groups. The specific surface area was measured using the BELSORP MINI II model device by nitrogen adsorptiondesorption BET analysis. UV-spectrophotometer (RAYLEIGH UV-2601) was used to assess the dye concentration.
## 2.5. Batch adsorption kinetics
Batch adsorption kinetic experiments were carried out to investigate the kinetic parameters and reach the equilibrium time. To carry out these tests, $0.05\mathrm{~g~}$ of each adsorbent beads was added to $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of methylene blue solution with $\mathsf{p H}=6.5$ and $8\mathrm{mg/L}$ initial concentration over a stirrer with $200\ \mathrm{rpm}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . At 0, 5, 10, 15, 30, 40, 60, 80, 120, and $240\mathrm{min}$ , a sample was prepared from each solution and the residual concentration of dye solution was measured by spectrophotometer at $664\mathrm{nm}$ wavelength [22]. The adsorbed dye on the adsorbents at t and equilibrium times were determined through the following equations [23]:
$$
\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}=\frac{(\mathsf C_{\mathrm{0}}-\mathsf C_{\mathrm{t}})\mathsf V}{\mathsf{m}}
$$
$$
\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}=\frac{(\mathsf C_{0}-\mathsf C_{\mathrm{e}})\mathsf V}{\mathsf{m}}
$$
where $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ and ${\sf q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ are the adsorbed dye at defined t and equilibrium times $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ , C0, $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}},$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ indicate the dye concentration in defined time t, initial and equilibrium times $\mathrm{\langlemg/L\rangle}$ ), V is the adsorptive solution (L), and m is the adsorbent mass (g). For the investigation, the effect of concentration on bath adsorption process, $0.05\mathrm{~g~}$ of each adsorbent beads was added to $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of methylene blue solution with $\mathrm{pH}=6.5$ and 4, 6, and $8~\mathrm{mg/L}$ initial concentration over a stirrer with 200 rpm at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After adsorption, the adsorbents were separated from the solution using a filter and the residual concentration of dye solution was measured by spectrophotometer. The kinetics models can provide information related to the adsorption paths and the involved mechanism. Adsorption kinetics controls the adsorption rate and determines the necessary time to achieve equilibrium in the adsorption process. This is one of the most valuable information for adsorption system design. Four models were used to determine kinetics parameters that are pseudo-first-order, pseudo-second-order, intra-particle infiltration, and Elovich models [24]. The pseudo-first-order kinetic model is based on this assumption that the adsorbent adsorption rate is directly related to the concentration difference between the fluid phase and the solid phase. The pseudo-first-order kinetic equation is stated as follows [24]:
$$
\mathrm{Log}({\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}-{\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}})=\mathrm{Log}({\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}})-\biggl(\frac{\mathrm{K}_{1}}{2.303}\biggr)\mathrm{t}
$$
where $\mathsf{K}_{1},\mathsf{q}_{\mathsf{t}},$ and ${\sf q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ are the pseudo-first-order equation constant (1/ min), the adsorbed dye at t $\left({\mathrm{mg/g}}\right)$ , and the adsorbed dye at equilibrium $\left({\mathrm{mg/g}}\right)$ , respectively [25]. The chemical adsorption that occurs at the solid level usually follows the pseudo-second-order kinetics. One of the important advantages of this model over the pseudo-first-order model is that its equilibrium capacity can be estimated from the model. The differential equation for the pseudo-second-order model is as follows:
$$
\frac{\mathrm{t}}{\mathrm{q_{t}}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{k_{2}}\mathrm{q_{e}^{2}}}+\left(\frac{1}{\mathrm{q_{e}}}\right)\mathrm{t}
$$
where ${\sf K}_{2}$ is the pseudo-second-order equation constant $(\mathrm{mg}/\$ $\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{min})$ [24]. The intra-particle infiltration kinetics model investigated the adsorbate mechanism in porous adsorbent and determine the rate controller. The linear form of this model is as follows:
$$
\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}=\mathsf{k}_{\mathrm{p}}\big(\mathsf{t}^{0.5}\big)+\mathsf{C}
$$
In the above equation, $\bf k_{\mathrm{p}}$ is intra-particle infiltration rate constant based on $(\mathrm{mg/g\cdotmin^{1/2}})$ and C is $\mathsf{y}\cdot\mathsf{\Lambda}\mathbf{y}.$ -intercept $\left({\mathrm{mg/g}}\right)$ . Elovich kinetics model is mostly executable for chemical adsorptions and systems with heterogeneous surfaces and its linear form is as follows:
$$
{\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}}=\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\right)\ \ln\left(\alpha\beta\right)+\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\right)\ \ln\left(\mathrm{t}\right)
$$
Here, $\upalpha$ is initial adsorption rate constant $(\mathrm{mg/g\cdotmin}^{\cdot}$ and $\upbeta$ is desorption constant $\left({\mathrm{mg/g}}\right)$ .
## 2.6. Dynamic adsorption kinetics
For a column adsorption process, the successful design required prediction of the breakthrough curves or concentration-time profiles for the effluent. The behavior of methylene blue in a fixed-bed column operation was studied to determine the breakthrough point at a constant temperature of $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Continuous adsorption tests were investigated in a polypropylene column with a height of $6\mathrm{cm}$ and a diameter of 4 mm. $0.05\mathrm{~g~}$ of each adsorbent was loaded into adsorption column and methylene blue solution with $\mathrm{pH}=6.5$ and $8~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ initial concentration over a flow rate of $0.74~\mathrm{mL/min}$ was added with the downward flow. In the interval of 0 to $350\mathrm{{min}}$ , effluent samples were analyzed to yield output concentration breakthrough curves. For the investigation, the effect of concentration on continuous adsorption process, $0.05{\mathrm{g}}$ of each adsorbent was loaded into the adsorption column and methylene blue solution with $\mathrm{pH}=6.5$ and 4, 6, and $8~\mathrm{mg/L}$ initial concentrations over a flow rate of $0.74~\mathrm{mL/min}$ was added with the downward flow. After reach adsorbent saturation, the concentration of the effluent dye solution was measured by spectrophotometer. The continuous adsorption kinetic experiments were studied in a fixed bed column system. Thomas, Adams-Bohart, Yoon-Nelson, Wolborska, and Modified Dose Response models are usually used to analyze the behavior of a continuous adsorption system. Thomas's model is the most common model widely used for continuous adsorption investigation. This dynamic model applies the second-order kinetics and equilibrium Langmuir models for the adsorbent saturation and considers plug flow behavior in the adsorption column. Thomas model can be represented by the equation as follows [26]:
$$
\ln\left({\frac{C_{0}}{C_{\mathrm{t}}}}{-1}\right)=\left({\frac{\mathrm{K_{th}q_{0}m}}{\mathrm{Q}}}\right){-\left({\frac{\mathrm{K_{th}}\ C_{0}\mathrm{V_{eff}}}{\mathrm{Q}}}\right)}
$$
$\mathrm{k_{TH,}C_{0},C_{t},q_{0,}m,Q}$ and $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{eff}}$ are influent dye concentration $\mathrm{(mg/L)}$ , effluent dye concentration $(\mathrm{mg/L})$ , the Thomas rate constant $\mathrm{(L/(min/mg)}$ ), equilibrium adsorption of dye $\left({\mathrm{mg/g}}\right)$ , adsorbent mass, volume flow rate $(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{min})$ , and effluent dye solution volume (L), respectively. Adam-Bohart model assumes that equilibrium adsorption is not instantaneous; therefore, the adsorption rate is proportional to the capacity of adsorption which remains on the adsorbent [25].
$$
\ln\left({\frac{C_{\mathrm{t}}}{C_{0}}}\right)=\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{AB}}\:C_{\mathrm{t}}\:{\mathrm{t}}-\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{AB}}\:\mathrm{N}_{0}\left({\frac{\mathrm{z}}{\mathrm{u}}}\right)
$$
Here, $\mathrm{k}_{\mathrm{AB,}}\ \mathrm{N}_{0,}\ Z,$ and u are the Bohart-Adams rate constant $(\mathrm{L}/\$ $\mathrm{{mg}\cdot\mathrm{{min}}}^{\cdot}$ , equilibrium concentration $\mathrm{(mg/L)}$ , adsorbent bed height (cm), and linear flow rate in column $\mathrm{(cm/min)},$ , respectively [25]. Yoon-Nelson model is a simple theoretical hypothesis that has not concentration on adsorbent properties, adsorbent type, and any physical properties of the adsorption bed. This model gives us a probabilistic description that a reduced adsorption rate is directly related to the adsorptive rate and pores on the adsorbent.
$$
\ln\left(\frac{C_{\mathrm{t}}}{C_{0}-C_{\mathrm{t}}}\right)=\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{YN}}\:\mathrm{t}-\tau\:\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{YN}}
$$
$$
\ln{\left(\frac{\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}}{\mathsf{C}_{0}}\right)}=\frac{\mathsf{\beta}\mathsf{C}_{0}}{\mathsf{N}_{0}}\mathsf{t}{-}\frac{\mathsf{\beta}\mathsf{Z}}{\mathsf{u}}
$$
in which $\upbeta$ is the kinetic coefficient of the external mass transfer $(1/\mathrm{{min}})$ and $\Nu_{0}$ is the exchange capacity $\mathrm{(mg/L)}$ . Modified Dose Response model diminishes the error resulting from the use of the Thomas model, particularly at lower or higher periods of the breakthrough curve [29].
$$
\ln\left({\frac{C_{\mathrm{t}}}{C_{0}-C_{\mathrm{t}}}}\right)=\sf{a}_{\mathrm{mdr}}\ln t-\sf{a}_{\mathrm{mdr}}\ln{(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{eff}}/\sf b_{\mathrm{mdr}})}
$$
$\mathsf{a}_{\mathrm{mdr}}$ and $\mathsf{b}_{\mathrm{mdr}}$ are the Modified Dose Response model constants [30].
## 2.7. Adsorbent regeneration tests
$0.05{\mathrm{g}}$ of each adsorbent beads was added to $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of methylene blue solution with $\mathsf{p H}=6.5$ and 4, 6, and $8~\mathrm{mg/L}$ initial concentration over a stirrer with $200~\mathrm{rpm}$ at $25~^{\circ}C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After adsorption, the adsorbents were separated from the solution using a filter and the residual concentration of dye solution was measured by spectrophotometer. For the regeneration process, the separated adsorbents were washed in 3 stages. In the first stage, they were washed by distilled water, in the second stage, they were washed by ethanol and in the third stage, and they were washed by distilled water. Then, the regenerated adsorbent was added to the adsorption process as mentioned above and this trend was repeated in 5 cycles.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology analysis
FESEM spectra were used to investigate the morphology and topography of the sample surface. The results indicate that hexagonal crystal nanoparticles with uniform particle size distribution Fig. 2b–d. According to Fig. 2e and f, the surface morphology of the beads appeared heterogeneous with the beads have roughness surfaces with holes and folds, which would be beneficial for the adsorption process. It can be said that these roughness surfaces are due to the presence of zeolites and ZIF-8 in the composite.
## 3.2. Investigating the stability of the prepared beads
To investigation of suitable concentration of cross-linker (iron ions) and CMC solutions for prepare stable beads, concentrations of 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.2, 0.5, 1, and $2\mathrm{M}$ of iron salt and 20, 40, and ${80}\mathrm{g/L}$ of CMC solutions were prepared. After preparing various concentrations of 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, $0.2\mathrm{M}$ of the iron solution and adding 20, 40, and $80\ \mathrm{g/L}$ of CMC solution, it was specified that beads at these lower concentrations of salt cannot be formed, while at concentrations of 0.5, 1, and $2\mathrm{{M}}$ of iron salt with 20 and $40\ \mathrm{g/L}$ of CMC solutions, the hollow beads were formed but did not show acceptable stability and after $24\mathrm{h}$ , they degrade in the water. Finally, the best stable hollow beads with a concentration of $2\mathrm{M}$ of iron salt and $80{\mathrm{g/L}}$ of CMC solutions resulted, and this amount was used to prepare the other beads (Fig. 3).
## 3.3. XRD analysis
Fig. 4 shows the XRD pattern of the synthesized ZIF-8, ZSM-5, CMC/ ZSM-5, CMC/ZIF-8, and CMC/ZSM-5/ZIF-8 samples. The ZSM-5 spectrum shows the characteristic peaks at $2\uptheta=23.1$ , 24.5, 24.9, and $30.2^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the JCPDS card no. 89–1421. The main diffraction peaks of the ZIF-8 sample can be observed at $2\uptheta=7.35$ , 10.4, 12.75, 18.1, 26.75, and 29.75 (JCPDS 00-062-1030) as seen in other similar works [31]. As shown in Fig. 4, by comparison with the standard component characteristic peaks, no significant change in the location of the characterization peaks occurred during the synthesis of the nanocomposite. Besides, a decrease in the intensity of the characteristic peaks of ZSM-5 and ZIF-8 can be seen with their addition to CMC, implying a decrease in the crystallinity of the CMC/ZSM-5, CMC/ZIF-8, and CMC/ZSM-5/ZIF-8 beads respect to ZSM-5 and ZIF-8.
## 3.4. FTIR analysis
FTIR analysis was used to identify the functional groups according to Fig. 5. In the infrared spectroscopy analysis, the spectrum at the absorption frequency of $422\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belongs to the Zn\ N bond. The spectrum at the absorption frequency of $689~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is related to the extracellular binding of the imidazole ring. The spectrum at the absorption frequency of $3448.29\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belongs to the tensile vibration of ${\sf N}\mathrm{-}{\sf H}$ in the imidazole organic ligand. The spectra at the absorption frequency of 1580 and $1305\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ relate to $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ bond and C\ N bond, respectively. The spectra at the absorption frequency 2926 and $3131~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are related to the tensile vibration of the symmetric bonds $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ and the tensile vibration of the asymmetric bonds $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ , respectively [32]. Additionally, the band in $790\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is related to Si\ O\ Si stretching vibration. The presence of some bands between 3300 and $3600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and at $1635~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are seen, which are attributed to the stretching vibrations of the $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ groups and the bending vibrations of the adsorbed water molecules, respectively [33–37]. On the other hand, the peaks of the components did not change significantly, indicating that the nanocomposite components are interconnected and combined. Only, the characteristic peaks of zeolite $3480~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and ZIF-8 $(3470~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) are shifted to lower wavenumber regions (3436 and $3440~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively) indicating the generation of hydrogen bonds between the –OH groups of CMC with ZIF-8 and zeolite.
## 3.5. BET analysis
The results of the BET analysis illustrate that ZIF-8 has a specific surface area, total pore volume, and average pore diameter of $1436.2\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}.$ $329.983\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , and $3.184\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Fig. 6a), which is significant and very appropriate compared to the values in previous studies ([38–42]), and shows that the synthesized ZIF-8 has a large special surface. Also, the figure indicates that adsorption and desorption of ZIF-8 follow the fourth-order isotherm [43,44]. Fig. 6a shows that the adsorption and desorption of ZIF-8 follow the type 4 isotherm. According to the BJH diagram, one can state that the synthesized ZIF-8 cavities are mesopores (Fig. 6b). In investigating the BET diagram of CMC/Zeolite/ ZIF-8 beads (Fig. 6c), the results show that this composite has a specific surface area of $21.51\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , the total pore volume of $0.032\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ , and average pore diameter of $5.88\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The resulted special surface for the beads is an acceptable number because for the more polymeric beads, this number is around 1 to $2\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . The decrease in specific surface area in the final nanocomposite indicates that some of the ZIF-8 nanocomposites are occupied by CMC molecule chains [45]. Additionally, the adsorption and desorption diagram shows that it follows the second-order isotherm type, and based on BJH charts, the synthesized beads are mesopore type (Fig. 6d).
## 3.6. Adsorption tests
## 3.6.1. Batch adsorption process
Batch adsorption tests were carried out to investigate the adsorption kinetics and achieve equilibrium time. In Fig. 7, the results show that methylene blue concentration decreases over time, and indeed, the adsorption rate decreases because the curve slope is declining to achieve equilibrium. Also, Fig. 7 shows that over time, dye adsorption value has been increased while achieving equilibrium and occupying the active site for adsorption. The batch adsorption process of methylene blue was investigated by the available models for batch adsorption and the results are presented in Fig. 7 and Table 1. $\mathsf{R}^{2}$ correlation coefficients show that all of the prepared adsorbents are consistent with the fitted models but they show more consistency with the pseudo-firstorder and intra-particle diffusion models and this shows that physical adsorption and intra-particle diffusion controlled the dye removal process.
## 3.6.2. Continuous adsorption process
Dynamic and continuous flow experiments of the adsorption process of methylene blue were investigated by all four adsorbents. The breakthrough curves illustrate the adsorption behavior of adsorptive-adsorbent in the column and are demonstrated by the ratio of effluent/influent dye concentration as a function of volume or time of effluent. The breakthrough curve configuration and the breakthrough occurring time are two important specifications of the kinetics of fixed-bed continuous adsorption. The results of the effluent dye concentration profiles and the obtained breakthrough curves represented that the effluent concentration is increased with the time, and meanwhile, the amount of the adsorbed dye inside the column is decreased, indicating that the active sites of the adsorbent are occupied and consequently, this leads to decreases reception capacity and increased adsorption. The experimental breakthrough curves were analyzed using different dynamic models of Bohart-Adams, Thomas, and Yoon– Nelson, Wolborska, and Modified Dose Response as presented in Table 2. The Bohart-Adams model describes based on first-order adsorption systems. Thomas's model is based on a pseudo-second-order surface reaction model for the adsorbate adsorption. Wolborska model is used for the description of the breakthrough curve in the range of the low concentration. Yoon-Nelson model incorporates terms for the surface adsorption rate and the breakthrough rates. Modified Dose Response model diminishes the error resulting from the use of the Thomas model, particularly at lower or higher periods of the breakthrough curve [25]. The continuous adsorption process of dyes by the synthesized adsorbent was shown an appropriate consistency with the dynamic models, in which the results can be seen in Fig. 8 and the fitting parameters are shown in Table 2 although the column data best fitted to the Thomas and Yoon–Nelson models.
The equilibrium adsorption capacity $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ $(\mathrm{mg/g})$ can be obtained using the following equation for batch and continuous processes, respectively:
$$
\mathsf{q_{e}}=\mathsf{V}\frac{(\mathsf C_{0}-\mathsf C_{\mathrm{e}})}{\mathsf{m}}
$$
$$
\displaystyle\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}=\frac{\mathsf{Q}\displaystyle\int_{\mathrm{t}=0}^{\mathrm{t}=\mathrm{t}}(\mathsf{C}_{0}-\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}})\mathrm{d}\mathrm{t}}{1000\mathrm{m}}
$$
where ${\sf q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the equilibrium adsorption capacity $({\mathrm{mg/g}})$ , $\mathsf{v}$ is solution volume (L), Q is the flow rate $\left(\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min}\right)$ , ${\sf C}_{0}$ is initial influent dye concentration $\mathrm{(mg/L)}$ , $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}$ is effluent dye concentration in defined time t $\mathrm{(mg/L)}$ ), $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ is effluent dye concentration in equilibrium time t $(\mathrm{mg/L})$ , and m (g) is mass of the adsorbent. The equilibrium batch adsorption capacities for CMC, CMC/ZIF-8, CMC/ZSM-5, and CMC/ZSM-5/ZIF-8 adsorbents were 12.01, 13.06, 11.53, and $10.49\mathrm{mg/g}$ respectively. Also, the equilibrium continuous adsorption capacities for CMC, CMC/ZIF-8, CMC/ZSM-5, and CMC/ZSM-5/ZIF-8 adsorbents were 10.56, 11.87, 9.29, and $8.15~\mathrm{mg/g}$ respectively. It can be concluded that although the equilibrium adsorption capacities in different processes represented results in the same ranges, ZIF-8 has a positive effect, and ZSM-5 has a negative effect on the dye adsorption process. The adsorption mechanism of a contaminant on an adsorbent is depending on various factors, counting the adsorbent properties, the adsorbate nature, and the probable adsorbate/adsorbent interactions. The methylene blue adsorption onto the bead can be involved valency forces through the sharing or exchange of electrons between methylene blue and beads. We can reasonably conclude that the functional groups present in the beads are involved in the adsorption of methylene blue molecules via both hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interaction. The investigations indicated that CMC was involved in the methylene blue adsorption process and –COOH groups for CMC play an important role in the adsorption as active sites for further methylene blue molecules attachment. The negatively charged carboxylate groups on the surface of CMC can bind with the positively charged groups in methylene blue through electrostatic interaction. Additionally, as methylene blue molecules contain $\mathsf{C}{\=}{\=}{\mathsf{C}}$ double bonds and benzene rings with π electrons, it is favorably that methylene blue molecule interacts with aromatic rings of the bead through π–π stacking. With the above background in mind, we can reasonably conclude that both electrostatic interactions, π–π stacking and hydrogen bonding are presumably involved in the adsorption mechanism. Besides, it has been reported that ionic bonding between the negatively charged functional groups of methylene blue $\left(-{\sf S O}_{3}^{-}\right)$ and positively charged center of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{OH})+$ can be formed. Besides, the formation of ionic bonding between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in ZIF-8 and $-S0_{3}^{-}$ might be the main contribution to the methylene blue adsorption on ZIF-8. The addition of ZSM-5 might decrease the active sites of dye adsorption with interaction with functional groups available on CMC and ZIF-8 and consequently decrease the methylene blue adsorption. Similar findings were also reported in previous works [46–50].
On the other hand, batch adsorption processes illustrated better results from continuous adsorption processes that it can be a result of more interaction between adsorbent and solution in the batch adsorption process. According to Fig. 9 for both adsorption processes, it can be said that increased concentration leads to increased ${\sf q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ . In fact, increased input solution concentration led to increased dye adsorption, because the driving force is increased. The increased concentration provides the driving force for adsorption process and increases the adsorption capacity.
The adsorption capacity of the prepared biocomposites was compared with some similar adsorbents, which represented that these beads have a significant ability to methylene blue removal from aqueous solutions besides the benefit of simple separation of them from the dye solution after adsorption process (Table 3).
## 3.7. Investigating adsorbent regeneration
Regeneration of the adsorbent is an important factor after dye adsorption because it allows reusing the adsorbent. Adsorbent regeneration tests indicate that the synthesized bionanocomposites can be used again after the adsorption process cycles. As shown in Fig. 10, adsorbent regeneration tests were carried out for all four adsorbents in five consecutive cycles. The results showed that the adsorption capacity of the adsorbents does not show a significant reduction, but in the next cycles, the adsorption capacity is more than the previous steps. In this study, ethanol was used as the regenerative. Indeed, these adsorbents with ethanol increase adsorption capacities after each regeneration cycle. It can be mentioned that ethanol is a wonderful matter to regenerate adsorbents because methylene blue is a basic dye and ethanol is an amphoteric acid that functions as an acid and electrostatic adsorbent increased adsorption capacity [51].
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, we investigate a novel process for the cultivation of self-assembled carboxymethyl cellulose hollow beads without the use of a core-template which yields spherical hollow beads based on metal-ion derivatized carboxymethyl cellulose prepared by partial etherification of the cellulose hydroxyl groups, contains metal-binding carboxylate groups and a large fraction of hydroxyl groups capable of forming hydrogen bonds. Here, carboxymethyl cellulose-based ZSM-5/ zeolitic imidazolate framework (CMC/ZSM-5/ZIF-8) hollow bionanocomposite beads with different compositions (CMC, CMC/ZIF8, CMC/ZSM-5 zeolite, and CMC/ZIF-8/ZSM-5 zeolite composites) were synthesized and employed as an adsorbent for methylene blue removal from aqueous solution in batch and continuous fixed bed systems. FESEM, FTIR, XRD, and BET measurements have been employed for characterizing the synthetic bionanocomposite and these techniques indicated that the bionanocomposites have been favorably synthesized. The kinetics, concentration effect on adsorption processes, and regeneration and reuse investigations were performed. Maximum batch adsorption capacities for CMC, CMC/ZIF-8, CMC/ZSM-5, and CMC/ZSM-5/ ZIF-8 adsorbents were 12.01, 13.06, 11.53, and $10.49\mathrm{mg/g}$ respectively. The batch adsorption process was investigated using pseudo-firstorder, pseudo-second-order, intra-particle diffusion, and Elovich kinetic models and the results showed that all four adsorbents are consistent with all models but the pseudo-first-order model showed more consistency. Maximum continuous adsorption capacities for CMC, CMC/ZIF-8,
CMC/ZSM-5, and CMC/ZSM-5/ZIF-8 adsorbents were 10.56, 11.87, 9.29, and $8.15\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively. The continuous adsorption process was investigated by Thomas, Adam-Bohart, Yoon-Nelson, Wolborska, and Modified Dose Response kinetic models and the results showed that the adsorbents showed more consistency with models of Thomas, Yoon-Nelson, and Modified Dose Response. Also, the generation process was successfully assessed in five steps.
